+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Shipped              |
+
+`ToolRegistry.execute` is the one place a model's tool call is checked. Every tool declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` that is handed verbatim to both providers, and the registry validates what the model sends back against that same schema before the executor runs, so a tool author declares the contract once and no consumer re-implements it differently or not at all. A call the schema rejects comes back to the model as a readable tool result it can correct within the existing tool-round budget, rather than as an exception that ends the conversation in a generic transport error.
+
+## The execute contract
+
+- `ToolRegistry.execute` validates `input` against the resolved definition's `inputSchema` before the executor runs; input the schema rejects never reaches the executor, and the rejection is returned as a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the failed constraint -- for a missing required property, that the property is required ([validated by: rejects input missing a required property, names it, and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L25)).
+- Input the schema accepts is passed through to the executor unchanged and exactly once, unlisted extra properties included -- the registry coerces no type, injects no default and strips no key ([validated by: passes accepted input through to the executor unchanged, extra properties included, exactly once](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L41)).
+- A wrong-typed field is rejected with a message naming the property path and the expected type, and the message carries no property value ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed field naming the path and expected type but not the value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L57)).
+- An `enum` constraint declared on a property is enforced, and the rejection names the property that failed it ([validated by: enforces a declared enum constraint and names the property it failed](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L74)).
+- Neither a rejection message nor a log line carries a property value from the rejected input: messages and log lines name property paths and constraints only ([validated by: keeps property values out of rejection messages and log lines](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L170)).
+- An unregistered name still throws `Unknown tool: <name>` -- the one throw left in `execute`, deliberately unchanged ([validated by: still throws Unknown tool for an unregistered name](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L164)).
+
+## Compiling schemas
+
+- A schema that cannot compile fails at `register` time, before a conversation can call the tool, and the thrown message names the tool ([validated by: throws at register time for a schema that cannot compile, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L98)).
+- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` is resolved per call, so every call is validated against the schema the source returns for that call rather than a stale one ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema current at each call](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L119)).
+- A static tool's schema is compiled once, at `register`, and the one compiled validator is reused across `execute` calls ([validated by: compiles a static schema once at register and reuses it across ten execute calls](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L140)).
+
+## In the tool loop
+
+- A rejected tool call does not throw out of `processMessage`: the rejection text is handed back to the provider as the tool result of the next round, and a corrected retry resolves within the same round budget ([validated by: returns a rejected tool call to the provider and resolves through the corrected retry](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts#L29)).
